  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.5" extension="2015-08-01"/><templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.5.1" extension="2015-08-01"/>
    <code code="11450-4" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Problem List"/>
    <title>Problems</title>
    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Bulimia nervosa, severe: recurrent binge eating, each time with self-induced vomiting, averaging about ten episodes a week for about three years, hidden from everyone; self-worth tied closely to body shape and weight; no laxatives, diuretics, diet pills, or excessive exercise. SCOFF 4 of 5; EDE-Q global score 3.9. Findings: bilateral parotid enlargement, enamel erosion on the inner surfaces of the upper front teeth, calluses on the backs of two knuckles of the right hand, a pulse rise of 22 per minute on standing, low potassium and chloride with a raised bicarbonate, and a raised amylase; BMI 19.3. Synthetic modern episode linked to her own public account of bulimia</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>F50.23</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis by DSM-5-TR criteria; severity is graded by the average number of compensatory episodes a week (8 to 13 is severe).</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Hypokalemia (potassium 3.1 mmol/L) with low chloride and metabolic alkalosis from repeated vomiting; QTc 452 ms; no palpitations, weakness, or fainting</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>E87.6</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting laboratory finding; resolved when potassium was normal on two checks.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Major depressive disorder, recurrent, moderate: about two months of low mood, tiredness, poor sleep, and feeling worthless and hopeless, with little pleasure in things she used to enjoy; PHQ-9 14 with item 9 scored 0; an earlier episode of depression after childbirth. Synthetic modern episode linked to her own public account of depression</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>F33.1</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis; the earlier episode is the post-natal depression she described publicly.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

