    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Hypokalemia from vomiting (E87.6)</content>. Potassium 3.6 mmol/L, from 3.1, on oral replacement with a little less vomiting; chloride 99 and bicarbonate 28 mmol/L, creatinine 0.80 mg/dL. Normal once; one more normal check is needed before the supplement stops. [S]<list><item>Continue potassium chloride 20 mEq twice daily.</item><item>Electrolytes again at the next visit.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Bulimia nervosa, severe (F50.23)</content>. Seven episodes a week, still in the severe range. Dr. Rosenthal agrees with the diagnosis and that outpatient care is safe: potassium is improving, the QTc was 452 ms at intake, the pulse rise on standing is 16 per minute, and she is not fainting. She advises eating-disorder-focused CBT (CBT-E) rather than guided self-help because the illness is severe and has lasted years, and fluoxetine from the start because of its severity and her depression. [S]<list><item>CBT-E with the eating disorders therapist: 20 sessions over 20 weeks, twice weekly for the first four weeks, starting this week.</item><item>Start fluoxetine 20 mg each morning, 40 mg after one week, and 60 mg after two weeks.</item><item>Nurse phone check in ten days for side effects, agitation, and suicidal thoughts.</item><item>ECG two weeks after reaching 60 mg.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Major depressive disorder, recurrent, moderate (F33.1)</content>. PHQ-9 13, from 14. Fluoxetine will treat the depression as well. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide or self-harm; the safety plan and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline are in her phone; no firearm. [S]<list><item>Call the clinic or 988 for any new agitation, restlessness, or thoughts of self-harm after starting fluoxetine.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Binge-eating and vomiting frequency (her self-monitoring record)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seven episodes of binge eating with vomiting this week, from about ten</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Eating Disorder Examination Questionnaire (EDE-Q) global score</td><td>Baseline, mid-treatment, and end of treatment</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Global score 3.9 at intake; repeat at mid-treatment</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight and BMI</td><td>Every visit, and weekly in CBT-E sessions</td><td>Completed today</td><td>61.2 kg, BMI 19.3</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Lying and standing pulse and blood pressure</td><td>Every visit while purging continues</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Lying 104/66, pulse 68; standing 100/64, pulse 84 (rise 16)</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Potassium, chloride, bicarbonate, and creatinine</td><td>Weekly until potassium is normal twice, then at each medical visit while purging continues</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Potassium 3.6 mmol/L, from 3.1; sodium 139, chloride 99, bicarbonate 28 mmol/L; creatinine 0.80 mg/dL, eGFR 103</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Magnesium and phosphate</td><td>Baseline, and again while potassium is low</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Magnesium 1.8 and phosphate 3.4 mg/dL at intake; magnesium again at the next visit</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>ECG with QTc</td><td>Baseline with purging or low potassium; after a QT-relevant medicine reaches its full dose while potassium has been low</td><td>Due</td><td>QTc 452 ms at intake; repeat two weeks after reaching fluoxetine 60 mg</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Parotid and dental examination; dental protection</td><td>Every visit; a dental examination soon after diagnosis and regular dental reviews while vomiting continues</td><td>Due</td><td>Parotid enlargement and enamel erosion unchanged; dental examination advised at intake, not yet booked</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Oral potassium supplement</td><td>While potassium is low or vomiting is frequent</td><td>Completed today</td><td>20 mEq twice daily, every dose taken; continue until a second normal check</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Fluoxetine dose, adherence, and side effects (nausea, sleep, agitation, sexual effects, suicidal thoughts)</td><td>At the start, one to two weeks after starting or a dose change, then every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Starting today at 20 mg; side effects, agitation and suicidal thoughts explained; nurse phone check in ten days</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Eating disorders psychiatry consultation</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Rosenthal today</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>CBT-E attendance and progress</td><td>Up to 20 sessions over 20 weeks, twice weekly for the first four weeks</td><td>Ordered</td><td>CBT-E starts this week</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Regular eating and dietitian review</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Breakfast on three days; dietitian seen once</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression measure (PHQ-9)</td><td>Monthly during acute treatment, then periodically</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-9 13, item 9 scored 0</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide-risk review, safety plan, lethal means, and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No thoughts of suicide or self-harm; safety plan reviewed; no firearm</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Level-of-care review against thresholds for urgent or inpatient care</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Outpatient care remains safe: potassium above 3.0 mmol/L, QTc below 500 ms, no fainting, pulse above 50</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>No binge eating or vomiting for at least four weeks by the end of 20 weeks of CBT-E. In progress; target 1990-07-13 [S]</item><item>Potassium at least 3.5 mmol/L on two checks within four weeks. In progress; target 1990-03-16 [S]</item><item>Regular eating (three meals and two or three snacks) on most days by three months. In progress; target 1990-05-11 [S]</item><item>PHQ-9 score below 5 by the end of treatment. In progress; target 1990-07-13 [S]</item><item>EDE-Q global score below 2.0 by the end of treatment. In progress; target 1990-07-13 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: The American Psychiatric Association Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Patients With Eating Disorders, Fourth Edition (2023); Eating disorders: recognition and treatment (NICE guideline NG69) (2017, updated 2020); Depression and Suicide Risk in Adults: Screening (2023); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: CBT-E twice weekly from this week. Nurse phone check in ten days. Visit in four weeks with electrolytes, magnesium, ECG, and the PHQ-9. Next scheduled visit 1990-03-16: five-week review: potassium and ECG on fluoxetine 60 mg, CBT-E progress, mood, and safety. [S]</paragraph></text>

